WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.380
I remember when we were little, we had . . . Well, we weren't little, little.

2
00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:17.960
But we were coming up and we used to have a rope tied in a tree at my cousin's house next door.

3
00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:22.300
We would all go over there, and we would run and catch the rope and swing.

4
00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:31.420
And one time my sister, she missed the rope and fell on her arm and broke her arm,

5
00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:38.060
and so then we all . . . It was a group of us, I’d say about ten to fifteen of us that would do that.

6
00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:40.020
We would do that every day.

7
00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:47.580
So when she broke her arm, well, we didn’t know what it was, but her arm was dented in.

8
00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:55.340
So we all was just trailing behind her, going to the house to tell them that she was hurt.

9
00:00:55.340 --> 00:01:01.520
And I remember they took her to the hospital and she came back, she had the cast on her arm.

10
00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:12.000
And the thing that I remember mostly about that was because all the parents was there with the children waiting to see what had happened,

11
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.100
what was wrong, and everything.

12
00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:22.340
I think about that because I think about how these days, people sue for everything.

13
00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:23.820
And it wasn't none of that.

14
00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.700
Nothing like that.

15
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:34.500
The rope came down though, but it was just still how good everybody was so concerned.

16
00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:41.120
But that was because of the closeness of the people.

17
00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:48.820
It brings to my memory these days when people tell me it takes a community to raise a child.

18
00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:51.740
And that's what that was like.

19
00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:55.360
Anybody in the community could chastise you.

20
00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.940
They didn't have to call your parents and tell you.

21
00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:07.820
They would chastise you at the point, and then you’d go home and you would get another scolding.

22
00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:26.520
But it was just that. . . We were just such a close-knit community that really we just . . . It just seemed like we didn't like it when all these other people start moving in because you didn't know anybody.

23
00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:34.220
Because before this, I mean, we could . . . You could leave your doors open at night.

24
00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:39.140
You just wasn't concerned because you knew nothing was going to happen.

25
00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:44.660
But then after people started moving in, then you had to start being careful.

26
00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:48.560
And it just . . . It really changed the way. . .

27
00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:49.300
Was that in the fifties?

28
00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:50.300
Pardon?

29
00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:51.540
Oh, yeah.

30
00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:51.740
Yeah.

31
00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:54.040
That's the forties, fifties.

32
00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:55.320
Well, thirties really.

33
00:02:55.320 --> 00:03:04.020
Yeah, so it was . . . Things change, you know.

34
00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:05.020
People want to advance.

35
00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:08.240
They want new things to happen.

36
00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:14.880
So Mossville wouldn’t get left behind, That's what happened.

37
00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:18.800
And it just . . . We just had to adjust.
